`main` is a live, moving branch that interleaves post-MVP proposal files
(GC, exceptions, tail calls, SIMD, threads/atomics) with plain WASM 1.0
MVP-core ones, so a pinned SHA -- not `main` itself -- is vendored to keep
this repo's conformance baseline reproducible across re-fetches. Bumping
the pin is a deliberate, reviewed decision, not something a re-run of the
fetch script does casually.

Mostly WASM 1.0 MVP-core `.wast` files are vendored here; see
`code/specs/W05-wasm-conformance-harness.md` section 6 for the original
exclusion rationale (SIMD, threads, exceptions, tail calls, bulk memory
operations, GC/reference-types beyond this repo's existing narrow slice,
memory64, the component model, and files needing the `spectest`
host-import module) -- still the same list after the WASM08 slice
extension, just a larger set of files that satisfy it. WASM18 (`code/
specs/W09-wasm-atomics-plain.md`) is the first deliberate exception:
`atomic.wast` exercises PLAIN atomic memory ops (load/store/RMW/
cmpxchg/fence), which don't need real multi-threading to be meaningful
-- `memory.atomic.notify`/`wait32`/`wait64`, in that same file, remain
genuinely out of scope and are graded `NotYetSupported`. Every other
excluded category above still becomes its own future phase's vendored
slice.

`linking.wast` (task #93) is a second deliberate exception: real cross-
module linking (imports/exports/`register` across separate `WasmInstance`s)
has had genuine support since WASM05, so the file's own original W05
exclusion note ("heavier module-linking semantics") no longer applies. Of
its 71 modules, exactly 2 import from `spectest` (this crate still has no
`spectest` host, by design) -- those two modules and whatever directives
depend on them grade `NotYetSupported`/collateral-fail; every other
directive exercises real, already-supported linking machinery.

`table_get.wast`/`table_set.wast` (task #96) surfaced (and this repo now
fixes) a real bug: table declarations never actually read their own
`funcref`/`externref` reftype keyword, silently defaulting every table's
element_type to funcref regardless of the source. Deliberately excludes
their sibling `table.wast` (a hex-literal table limit + a real `spectest`
import, task #99) and `table_size.wast`/`table_grow.wast`/`table_fill.wast`
(need `table.size`/`table.grow`/`table.fill`, entirely unimplemented
opcodes, task #98).

`memory_copy.wast`/`memory_fill.wast` (task #94) are the first vendored
files from the bulk-memory proposal. `memory.copy` already had a real
interpreter handler (E4-dyn's runtime string concat needed it); this
vendoring pass added `memory.fill`'s handler and text-form parsing
support for BOTH instructions (neither had any `wast-parser` support
before -- see that crate's own CHANGELOG). Deliberately excludes their
sibling `bulk.wast`: it mixes memory.copy/memory.fill with
memory.init/data.drop (task #95) and table.init/elem.drop/table.copy
(task #97) in the same file, all still unimplemented.

`memory_init.wast` (task #95) needed real new interpreter state --
`memory.init`/`data.drop` (0xFC 0x08/0x09) were entirely unimplemented
before this pass (no opcode decoding, no interpreter handler, no
wast-parser text-form support, no validator type-check rule), and
neither can work without real PASSIVE data segments (`(data $d
"bytes")`, no offset expression): `wasm-module-parser`'s binary decoder
only ever handled segment-mode flag `0x00` (active, implicit memory 0)
before this, not the real 3-mode encoding the bulk-memory proposal
defines. 100% pass, every directive kind, on the first baseline regen
after implementation. Only uses single-memory numeric segment indices
(no `spectest` import, no table/elem instructions), so it's vendorable
standalone -- unlike its sibling `bulk.wast` (task #97 still pending)
and `memory-multi.wast` (task #92, explicit multi-memory indices, was
blocked on real multi-memory memarg support).

`memory-multi.wast` (task #92/#109-112) is that follow-up, now unblocked:
every memarg-carrying load/store opcode (`0x28`-`0x3E`, 23 total) plus
`memory.copy`/`memory.fill`/`memory.init` previously hardcoded memory
index 0 and either ignored or assumed a fixed byte width for any memidx
immediate -- a real, decoded-but-discarded gap, not a deferred feature
(`ModuleContext.has_memory` was a plain bool for exactly this reason, per
its own doc comment). This pass added the real binary flags-bit `0x40` +
memidx decode/encode (`wasm-execution`), the matching text-form leading
memidx token for `i32.load`/`memory.fill`/`memory.init`
(`wasm-wast-parser`), and the matching validation-time bounds-check
(`wasm-validator`, `ModuleContext.memory_count`) -- see `code/specs/
W18-wasm-multi-memory-memarg.md`. 100% pass, every directive kind
(2/2 modules, 4/4 assert_return), on the first baseline regen after
implementation.

`simd_i32x4_arith.wast`/`simd_i32x4_cmp.wast` (task #113-117) widen the
SIMD first slice's opcode coverage (`code/specs/
W13-wasm-simd-v128-first-slice.md`) within the SAME `i32x4` lane width
it already covers -- no new lane width, no new binary-encoding shape,
same "highly mechanical once the lane-arithmetic helpers exist" pattern
that spec's own W07 sizing doc predicted. Adds `i32x4.mul`/`neg`/`sub`
(joining the already-implemented `add`) and the full comparison family
`ne`/`lt_s`/`lt_u`/`gt_s`/`gt_u`/`le_s`/`le_u`/`ge_s`/`ge_u` (joining
`eq`) -- 12 new opcodes, each sub-opcode byte fetched live from the SIMD
proposal's own `BinarySIMD.md` and cross-checked against the already-
implemented `i32x4.eq`(`0x37`)/`i32x4.add`(`0xAE`) entries (both matched
exactly, same verification discipline as the original 5-opcode slice).
`simd_i32x4_arith.wast`: 100% pass on every gradeable directive (2/2
modules, 181/181 assert_return, 11/11 assert_invalid).
`simd_i32x4_cmp.wast`: 100% pass on every gradeable directive (420/420
assert_return, 30/30 assert_invalid); its one `NotYetSupported` module
and 13 `NotYetSupported` assert_return directives exercise
`i32x4.trunc_sat_f32x4_s`/`f32x4.const` float-to-int truncation boundary
values, genuinely out of scope for this i32x4-only widening pass.
Deliberately excludes their sibling `simd_splat.wast`: it references many
more unsupported opcode families across ALL 6 lane-width shapes
(f32x4/f64x2/i16x8/i8x16 arithmetic, `shr_s`, `all_true`, `swizzle`,
saturating add/sub, `trunc_sat`), not just more `i32x4` ops, so it stays
deferred to a future, larger widening pass.

